{
  "_comment": "VOICE-AGENT-126: single source of truth for vocabulary shared by the voice (app.js) and text (main.py) paths. Edit here; both modes read it (Python at import, JS via window.__LEXICONS__ injected by the / route). See AGENTS.md.",
  "_comment_asr": "VOICE-AGENT-128: domain bias for the speech-to-text step (audio.input.transcription.prompt on the Realtime session, and the /transcribe upload). Without it the transcriber hears 'back surface' for 'box office' and 'the titan song list' for 'Sight and Sound'. Two lists on purpose: asr_domain_terms is the durable cinema vocabulary, asr_proper_nouns is what the CURRENT demos say out loud and is meant to be edited before a shoot. Keep both short: an over-long prompt makes the transcriber hallucinate the terms it was primed with.",
  "_comment_asr_intro": "The intro carried a French sentence until 2026-08-04, on the reasoning that VOICE-AGENT-102 had removed the language lock and an English-only prompt would tilt detection back toward English. Measured on a recording session, it does the opposite and worse: the transcriber TRANSLATED. 'Ok, show me the movies in the Criterion Collection' came back as 'Ok, montre-moi les films de la collection Criterion', a literal French rendering of the English line, twice in a row, and 'Now, only the dramas' came back as 'Non, only the dramas'. A prompt is a text sample, so a French sample makes a French output plausible for English audio. The intro is now language-neutral and says so explicitly; the proper nouns carry the domain bias on their own, being the same words in both languages.",
  "asr_prompt_intro": [
    "Conversation about cinema and television.",
    "Transcribe exactly what is said, in the language it is spoken. Never translate."
  ],
